WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.468 --> 00:00:03.180
Narrator: Some areas around
the house are easy to clean,

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.750
but it's often the out-of-sight
jobs that are the hardest.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.210
One of the most challenging aspects

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.980
of deep cleaning these spaces

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.440
is the buildup of grime over time.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.600
Air vents that haven't
been cleaned in roughly

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.260
half a century require
special tools, for example,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.897
and a roof that's covered in
nearly three decades of moss

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:25.080
calls for a skilled technician.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.200
Neglecting these spaces
can have consequences.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.602
The accumulation of debris
in these hard-to-reach areas

00:00:31.602 --> 00:00:34.320
can cause structural damage, spark fires,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.540
or even induce health hazards.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.200
Watch as eight specialists
share expert tips of the trade

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.910
and explain what it takes

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.033
to deep clean decades of household grime.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.330
Duct Heroes is an air-duct-cleaning
company in Illinois.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.517
Many of its clients have never done

00:00:53.517 --> 00:00:55.500
a single air-duct cleaning,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.020
and today's job is no exception.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.420
These vents haven't been cleared of dust

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.583
and debris for a century.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.450
Ahmad: As soon as we walked
in, we see the old vents,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.520
the furnace, how old it was.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.890
Before even opening any of the vents up,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.810
I knew how bad it was going to be.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.690
You know, he said 47 years of dust;

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.453
probably over 100 years, to be honest.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.530
If you look at a vent

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:27.420
and you see dust on the actual vent,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.590
that's a clear sign

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.230
that the ducts have
probably never been cleaned

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.420
and they're pretty filthy inside.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.850
We like to start on the
very top of the house

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.440
and work our way down.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.310
That tool that we're
using, we call it whiskers.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.422
The whiskers are just made out of rubber.

00:01:43.422 --> 00:01:45.630
The end of it's attached
to an air compressor,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.050
and you have air just
coming out through the rod

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.550
out the little whisker things.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.320
So, what you're looking at right now

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.710
is just me pulling chunks of dust out

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.980
that have probably been in
there for over 100 years.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.084
There was too much dust to
be putting it in the vacuum,

00:02:12.084 --> 00:02:15.000
so I literally went in there with my hands

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.360
and grabbed probably 5 pounds of dust.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.068
Isn't it gnarly? Look at
how much dust that is.

00:02:22.068 --> 00:02:24.950
It's just so sick, honestly.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.787
This is the back of the dryer,

00:02:38.787 --> 00:02:41.280
and you guys can pretty much see

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.043
there's an endless amount
of lint coming out.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.410
There's a few reasons

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.427
why you should get your
dryer vent cleaned.

00:02:48.427 --> 00:02:50.490
It's a huge fire hazard,

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.250
and your clothes will
dry more efficiently.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.010
It's well worth the money to just pay

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.785
to get it cleaned every year.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.878
The vacuum is on throughout
the whole process,

00:03:13.878 --> 00:03:15.900
and what we're doing is
blowing all that dust

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.483
and debris closer and
closer to the vacuum.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.513
This house in particular had 18 vents.

00:03:29.513 --> 00:03:31.688
Most of the time, I think,

00:03:31.688 --> 00:03:34.478
probably 75% of the time,

00:03:34.478 --> 00:03:37.893
the houses that we go to
have never been cleaned.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.880
People just don't think about it.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.642
Getting your air ducts cleaned isn't

00:03:43.642 --> 00:03:46.920
a regular thought that comes to your head.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.890
The dust you typically
see in your vents is,

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:53.790
most of it is actually human skin cells.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.923
Yeah, it's pretty gnarly.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.160
So, right now we're done,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.180
and everything's pretty much good to go.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.688
You're pretty much going to see

00:04:08.688 --> 00:04:11.738
a brand-new home, honestly.

00:04:11.738 --> 00:04:14.220
You're going to feel much better.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.900
Your health is going to be better.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.720
I actually got a phone
call after that job,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.860
and they said all their
allergies went away.

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.653
It's just a huge health benefit, honestly.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.340
You might not notice the small stuff,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.873
but your body does.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.070
Narrator: Moving underground,
there are the sewage pipes,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.110
which after heavy use are in need

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.663
of significant cleaning and repairs.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.540
Instead of digging underground

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.490
or having to do any demolition

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.040
to replace or clean sewage pipes,

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.950
Kenneth Kaas cures them from the inside.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:56.673
He's a plumber from Norway who
specializes in pipe relining.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.787
This blue tube you're
seeing is being pushed

00:04:59.787 --> 00:05:02.880
through a faulty sewage
pipe using pressurized air,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.730
which will create a tough new
pipe inside of the old one.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.000
This technique can be used

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.180
to fix cracked, leaky, or old pipes.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.000
Some signs that a pipe needs
to be inspected include

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.960
sewer odors, slow drains,
and constant clogs.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.020
Pipe relining is a quicker
method compared to replacements,

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.603
and it only takes two
workers to complete the job.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.170
Workers start by inspecting
the inside of the pipe

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.311
with a tiny camera.

00:05:32.311 --> 00:05:34.383
They're looking for any cracks or leaks.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.703
Once they assess the damage,

00:05:38.703 --> 00:05:42.087
they'll clean the pipe with
water and a rotating chain.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.670
This breaks up any caked-on
rust, dirt, hair, sewage,

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.920
and oil inside the pipe.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.230
All this buildup shrank
the size of the pipe,

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.363
making it difficult for
waste to flow through it.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.590
Now that all that waste has been removed,

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.720
the pipe is back to its original size,

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.450
which will allow the new lining to stick.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.910
Now the relining process begins.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:07.260
The workers measure the pipe

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:10.694
and cut a piece of felt lining
to roughly the same size.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:24.150
A vacuum pump sucks out any
air from inside the felt

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.910
so that it can be filled with epoxy resin.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.773
They mix the resin by hand.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.000
Now that there's no room for air bubbles,

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.760
which weaken the resin, they fill it up.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.180
Epoxy resin is superstrong
and resistant to chemicals.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.550
It's also a good choice
to prevent roots of trees

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:51.210
or plants from getting in
the pipes and clogging them.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.138
And since resin starts as a liquid,

00:06:53.138 --> 00:06:55.803
it can be used to repair
pipes of any size.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:02.603
The team flattens the lining
and puts it in a compressor.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.100
The compressor uses pressurized air

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.792
to shoot the lining into the old pipe.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.493
Once it's in there, the lining is inflated

00:07:30.493 --> 00:07:32.850
so that it sticks to the walls.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.940
A new pipe starts to
form inside the old one.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.100
It cures and hardens for four hours.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:41.163
With the help of pressurized
air and heat from a steamer.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:45.178
Then the lining is deflated,

00:07:46.745 --> 00:07:49.353
trimmed, and removed,

00:07:49.353 --> 00:07:51.590
leaving behind a new pipe inside.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.650
The pipes can be used as
normal once the job is done,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.990
and no water will be able
to get in between the liner

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.313
and the original pipe.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.807
The finished product
can last over 50 years,

00:08:03.807 --> 00:08:06.873
which is twice as long
as a brand-new PVC pipe.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:13.050
Gutters are some of the
most important things

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:16.560
to keep clean, especially before winter.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.842
I'm Broden, and I own a
business in Connecticut

00:08:20.842 --> 00:08:21.900
called The Gutter Guyz.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.060
Today I'm going to show
you how to clean gutters

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:26.209
with wet and dry debris.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.600
So, when we clean a
gutter with wet debris,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.420
nine times out of 10, we
want to clean it by hand.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:43.530
The reason why is when it's
wet, it typically is very muddy

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:46.445
and there's a lot of what
we call shingle dust,

00:08:46.445 --> 00:08:49.860
or grit, in the gutters,
and it's literally a mud.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.230
And when we blow them when they're wet,

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:54.150
it makes a muddy mess all over the house.

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.450
We want to clean them
by hand for that reason.

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:02.400
So, that is a gutter that is
completely jammed with leaves

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.400
and debris, and the downspout
was completely covered.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.110
When we released the
debris out of the downspout

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.140
and got that gutter cleaned,
all of that rainwater

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.172
that was in the gutter just
started to flow very heavily

00:09:17.172 --> 00:09:19.534
right down the downspout, as it should.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.720
So, oftentimes when we
do a gutter cleaning,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.310
not only do we find leaves,
but we find plants so big

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.500
that we actually call
them trees, sometimes,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:32.970
growing right out of a gutter.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.820
We're actually pulling out a big plant,

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.560
and you can see the whole root system

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:38.710
come right out with it.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.830
Certain types of trees give
off these little things

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.740
that come off the tree and
will grow another tree.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.990
And when those land in the gutter,

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:52.290
it's a perfect environment
for that to grow.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.310
It's a classic sign

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:55.410
that your gutters have
not been cleaned in years.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.590
It also puts a significant
amount of weight

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.140
from the whole root system
growing in the gutter

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.140
on the gutter, which in turn will pull

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:06.300
that gutter off of the fascia board,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.760
which is actually the
board behind the gutter

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.413
that the gutter is mounted to.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:17.100
Wet debris is usually
just saturated with water.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.180
Water will overflow at the
gutter and get into your basement

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:24.000
and create cracks in your foundation.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.460
Once water does find its
way into your basement,

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:27.573
it wreaks havoc.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.420
Here, the water that's
flowing out of the downspout

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.900
at the bottom is the end
result that we are looking for.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:41.042
So that water will flow down
and out, as it properly should.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.608
When we see dry debris, the
reason why that debris is dry

00:10:49.608 --> 00:10:51.857
is because there has not been any rain

00:10:51.857 --> 00:10:54.390
in the past, I would say, week or so.

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:56.880
So, when we clean a gutter
that has dry debris,

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.100
oftentimes we will use a hand blower

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.570
and blow those gutters out

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:02.310
because it does a really good job

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.290
of just getting everything
out of the gutter,

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:06.180
whether it be leaves or sticks

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.730
or curly stuff that
comes off in the spring.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.140
Depending on the pitch of the roof,

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.630
we will get on that roof,
start our hand blower,

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.670
and walk across that roofline

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:18.033
hand-blowing out all of the
debris out of that gutter.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:23.970
Sometimes the debris is
very compact and it's thick.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:27.540
That's a classic sign of
maybe a couple of years

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.973
that somebody had not had
their gutters cleaned.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:32.970
We stick to one type of ladder,

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.440
and that's an aluminum ladder.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:36.990
The aluminum ladder does
conduct electricity,

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.420
so it's very important we
stay away from power lines

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.790
when we are moving aluminum ladders.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.430
We could get severely electrocuted.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:45.720
But certainly deadly,

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:49.230
which is why we mark the
power-line areas with flags

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:51.180
before we start a gutter cleaning,

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.770
because you're looking
where you're walking,

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:54.750
so you don't look up
to see the power lines.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.760
So it's very important
for us to have a reminder

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:58.560
that the power lines are there.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.738
We will often drop a
penny down the downspout

00:12:02.738 --> 00:12:05.296
just to know that that
downspout's free and clear

00:12:05.296 --> 00:12:07.989
because we can see and hear
it go all the way through

00:12:07.989 --> 00:12:09.720
and back out at the bottom,

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.803
and we know that we're in good shape.

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:17.248
The best advice I can give a homeowner

00:12:17.248 --> 00:12:19.680
is to be proactive and not reactive.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.840
Typically, when you're
reactive, it's too late.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.130
Damage has been done,

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.110
the water may already be in your basement,

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:26.820
it may already be in your home,

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:31.820
and it's just not a good
way to maintain your house.

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:35.670
Water is seriously
detrimental to your house,

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.550
and the main cause of
water is from your gutters.

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:41.610
So if you keep the gutters free and clear

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:44.640
and you do consistent
cleaning, whether it be two

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.980
or three times per year,
which we recommend,

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:50.310
you will never have any issues
with water in your basement

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.520
or ruining your flower beds
or rotting out the fascia

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:54.870
and soffits of your home.

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:56.670
Just stay on top of gutter cleaning.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:12:59.940
Narrator: Perhaps even worse

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.917
than a clogged gutter full of leaves

00:13:01.917 --> 00:13:05.160
is a pool that hasn't been
cleaned for six years.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.233
Watch as Miles Laflin cleans out

00:13:07.233 --> 00:13:10.860
one of the dirtiest pools he's ever seen.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.867
Miles: All right, then.
I had a DM come through.

00:13:12.867 --> 00:13:15.210
Can I help sort out this pool?

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:17.850
Which actually looks
more like a pond, I know.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:18.780
I was half expecting

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:20.910
to see a fish swimming
around in the bottom.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.760
There was plants growing
in there, like pond plants.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:26.055
They thought maybe when
they were cutting the grass

00:13:26.055 --> 00:13:29.246
and they had the doors open,
maybe little bits blew in

00:13:29.246 --> 00:13:31.620
and seeds or whatever
just went into the water

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.510
and bits started growing and stuff.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:35.880
So maybe like that. But who knows?

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.950
We're going to empty it and clean it out.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:40.260
Drop in the submersible pump first,

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:41.810
and we'll be back the next day.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.093
Nice and empty, but not nice and clean.

00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:47.970
Time to get to work.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.670
I'm going to jump in and have
a look at the damage first.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.140
Yeah, so once the pool was empty,

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:58.380
we brushed them in into a
corner with a big brush,

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:00.090
shoveled them out into a bucket,

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.160
and threw it on their compost heap.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:03.840
The main difference
between cleaning an indoor

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.763
and an outdoor pool is
normally you don't get

00:14:06.763 --> 00:14:09.330
any sort of debris in a indoor pool.

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:11.160
Obviously this one had
plants growing in it.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.800
Took about five or six years

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.210
for this pool to get like that.

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:16.410
But for an outdoor pool,

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.660
that would probably
only take maybe a year,

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.093
year and a half for it
to get like that state.

00:14:26.997 --> 00:14:30.420
So, before we emptied the pool,
this was actually floating,

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:32.520
so when we emptied it, it sort of, like,

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:35.320
the plants had started attaching
themselves to the wall.

00:14:40.871 --> 00:14:43.122
Then jet-washing the walls and the floor.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:49.730
Quickly have a guess at what picture's

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:51.462
tiled in on the floor.

00:14:51.462 --> 00:14:55.230
I went for a dolphin.
Turns out it's a sea lion.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:56.423
That's pretty cool, though.

00:14:58.707 --> 00:14:59.540
It's coming up nicely,

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.702
but there's still some stains left over.

00:15:01.702 --> 00:15:03.180
So what we're going to
do is mix up some acid

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:04.473
in a bucket of water,

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:07.640
scrub that all over the floor and walls,

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.160
then we're going to jet wash the surround,

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:16.323
jump back in the pool, rinse that down,

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.500
get the big Hoover out,

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:21.809
start Hoovering up all
the last little bits.

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:48.930
So, that is a chlorine dispenser,

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:53.210
and you can fill that up
with the chlorine tablets,

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:55.410
and it slowly dissolves the tablets,

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:57.513
feeding chlorine into the pool.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:03.480
You could fill up the chlorine
feeder with the tablets,

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.060
but it won't give your
pool instant chlorine.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:08.670
You'd then put in the granules to give it

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:11.482
that initial chlorine dose,

00:16:11.482 --> 00:16:13.223
and then the tablets will slowly dissolve,

00:16:13.223 --> 00:16:15.131
bringing up the chlorine level as well.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.035
That is,

00:16:24.035 --> 00:16:26.911
well, some people think it's
me adding color to the pool.

00:16:26.911 --> 00:16:29.947
[laughs] "Is that what
makes the pool look blue?"

00:16:29.947 --> 00:16:32.640
is what some people message me.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.010
But actually it's algaecide,

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:38.433
and that is to prevent
the growth of algae.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.940
So, that is a jelly cube,

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:49.260
and it's a water clarifier.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.950
Also, it removes phosphates from the pool

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:55.740
and helps with chemical efficiency.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.692
What it does is help put, like, a layer,

00:16:57.692 --> 00:16:59.250
pick up small particles,

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:01.290
therefore making the pool a bit clearer.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:05.250
When you initially put salt in a pool,

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:09.030
a freshly filled pool, it
will take around a week

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:13.110
for the salt to be generated
into chlorine itself,

00:17:13.110 --> 00:17:15.743
and the process is called electrolysis.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:33.370
It looked as if it had just been finished

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:35.277
from, like, new build.

00:17:35.277 --> 00:17:37.482
The tiles were nice and blue,

00:17:37.482 --> 00:17:40.353
nice and white tiles, very clean looking.

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.550
100 times better than what
it did when I turned up.

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:48.540
This was all within
maybe half a day's work.

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:50.040
I mean, I've seen green indoor pools,

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.340
but never an indoor pool
with anything growing

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:54.807
other than a little bit of algae.

00:17:58.723 --> 00:18:01.290
Narrator: A home's
exterior is best cleaned

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.060
with a good power washer.

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:04.350
My name's Aj Joyner,

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.660
owner of Advanced Pressure Washing LLC.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.510
Today I'm going to show you
how we clean the exterior

00:18:09.510 --> 00:18:12.677
of a client's dirty home step by step.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.890
We, in general, we
offer exterior cleaning,

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:19.020
but the main services
in the residential field

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:23.700
are house washing, gutter
clean-out, concrete cleaning.

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:26.490
Those are the main-ticket
items that we solicit

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:28.074
to the residential clients.

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.980
The condition of the fence
structurally is great,

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:40.783
but as you can see, it's
covered in mildew and algae.

00:18:40.783 --> 00:18:42.000
Wood is very particular

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.400
because if you use too much pressure,

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:46.980
you're going to cause a lot
of damage to the surface.

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:48.960
So to treat it, we just use the water.

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:51.000
We actually use moderate pressure,

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.310
and we make sure always
to go with the grain.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:56.610
This keeps us from
furring/damaging the wood.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:15.090
The driveway is terrible!
Covered in mildew and algae.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:16.710
The chemicals we normally use

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:19.680
is called sodium hypochlorite, 12.5%,

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:24.210
or better known as pool
shock/industrial bleach.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:25.530
We normally dilute that down

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.870
to a 50:50 ratio in our buckets.

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:29.760
This kills the mildew and algae,

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:31.980
that top layer, when we pretreat.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:33.980
It kills that almost instantly.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:41.850
This is called a Whisper
Wash Big Guy surface cleaner.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:44.033
That metal tubing actually

00:19:44.033 --> 00:19:46.616
rotates around on a swivel.

00:19:46.616 --> 00:19:49.990
That's the item that I use
to actually clean concrete.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:08.040
From there, we'll actually
go around washing the trim

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.585
around the brick and other areas

00:20:09.585 --> 00:20:11.926
the surface cleaner couldn't reach,

00:20:11.926 --> 00:20:14.351
and then we will post-treat
with the detergent again

00:20:14.351 --> 00:20:17.650
to get any organic stains
that are left behind.

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:24.180
After we do the driveway,

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:27.960
we'll then do the front
patio and sidewalk.

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:29.790
Also sometimes, if the client wants,

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:32.757
we'll also do the curves along the drive.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:06.210
The house is in pretty good condition,

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:07.860
but there was a few spots that we noticed

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:12.090
that were pretty bad with
heavy, thick mildew and algae.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:16.916
On a house, we use roughly
about 10% sodium hypochlorite

00:21:16.916 --> 00:21:20.130
mixed with about 90% water.

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:22.080
So, the black on the brick is actually

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.420
just really thick mildew and algae.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:27.510
This is caused by moisture and shade,

00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:30.944
allowing for such big
mildew and algae to grow.

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.980
When we wash a house's
exterior, we wash the roof,

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:50.310
the outside, the gutters,
beside the home, the windows,

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:53.370
the doors, anything that is on the side

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:56.583
or attached to the home itself.

00:22:10.650 --> 00:22:13.410
So, we're finished. We
finally got the job done.

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:18.030
So, roughly this job
took us about 4 ½ hours.

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:19.530
Everything looks amazing.

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:22.920
The client was beyond happy
with the job that we have done.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:26.220
You can clearly see
everything is transformed

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:28.320
into a new clean state.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.720
My personal suggestion, instead of trying

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:33.690
to do it yourself, just
always hire professionals

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:35.400
because you're dealing
with some very strong

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:38.580
and dangerous chemicals,
and it can become dangerous

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:41.780
when in the hands of
someone not experienced.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:49.800
Narrator: Next up, Sid Partridge

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:52.958
of Partridge Exterior Cleaning in the UK

00:22:52.958 --> 00:22:56.010
walks us through what it takes
to remove moss from a roof

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:59.403
that hasn't been touched
in nearly three decades.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:02.585
Sid: Here I'm beginning
to scrape the roof,

00:23:02.585 --> 00:23:05.520
so I'm trying to remove
as much moss as possible.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:07.440
This is what I'll be doing
for the bulk of the day,

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:09.780
and this is the hardest part of the job.

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:10.950
I have the tool that I use.

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:14.490
It's basically a flat metal
blade angled to one side,

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:16.680
which means I can get
the edge of the tile,

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:18.750
which usually carries a lot of the moss,

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:22.200
and scrape the bulk surface
of the tile at the same time.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:23.790
I use sort of a side-to-side motion,

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.400
and it is very taxing on the
body, so it takes a long time

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.670
to build up the endurance
and strength to do it.

00:23:29.670 --> 00:23:31.980
So, on this roof you'll see
a lot of brown and green.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:33.300
This is the moss. You'll also see a lot

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:35.670
of white spots people
think is bird droppings,

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:38.083
but this is white lichen.

00:23:38.083 --> 00:23:39.510
Moss is terrible for roofing

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.090
and roof materials, especially in the UK,

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:44.040
as we get a lot of damp weather over here,

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.660
so it just tends to grow and fester more,

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.000
and it'll grow in between
the cracks of the tiles.

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:50.100
Now, when you have hot weather
turn into cold weather,

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:51.810
the moss will freeze and expand

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:53.220
and then end up cracking the roof tiles,

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:55.080
which can create holes, leaks,

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.790
and things like that in the roof.

00:23:56.790 --> 00:23:58.110
So, this part of the job,

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:00.210
where I'm on the
scaffolding, was the hardest.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:02.580
So, I used a 40-feet extendable pole

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.290
with the scraper attached on the end,

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:06.420
and I was almost at max extension.

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:08.250
So, the scraper itself is quite heavy,

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:10.140
and the pole itself is quite heavy.

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:12.420
So having that out at
a near horizontal level

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:14.700
for a couple of hours is very difficult.

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:16.530
And as soon as this was
done, I was relieved

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:18.870
and happy to move on to the next part.

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:22.560
So, what I'm cleaning here
is a four-bedroomed house.

00:24:22.560 --> 00:24:24.810
It's got big flat concrete tiles.

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.760
So, these are some of the
best tiles to come across

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:28.500
to clean the surface area of them.

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:30.030
And because they're completely flat,

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:31.650
it's very easy to get the moss off.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.660
You do not get roofs better than this.

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:35.580
So, it's rare that you'll
actually get away with

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:37.718
using a broom to clean a roof,

00:24:37.718 --> 00:24:39.910
as usually the moss is
very, very stuck on,

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:41.993
and it's typically just
used for cleaning up.

00:24:41.993 --> 00:24:43.980
However, on this job, the
moss was coming off easy.

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.410
So in certain sections
it can just be easier

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:48.150
just to try and sweep off a majority

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:50.642
and then sort of fine-tune
it with a scraper.

00:24:55.931 --> 00:24:57.360
So, the typical cleaning chemicals

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:58.830
that I use in my roof-cleaning work

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:01.440
is a chemical called a biocide.

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:02.925
What this essentially will do

00:25:02.925 --> 00:25:05.790
is destroy any of the organic
growth left on the roof,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.430
and it has a residual cleaning
effect once I'm gone as well.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:10.320
So there's no rinsing it
off; you leave it on the roof

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:12.545
to fully dry and soak into the tiles.

00:25:12.545 --> 00:25:14.790
This will get anything that
we can't get to by scraping.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.447
So anything that's left in the gaps.

00:25:16.447 --> 00:25:17.970
Any sort of really stubborn stains

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.620
and marks that are left on the tiles

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:21.300
also will slowly degrade.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:24.210
So, the brush that I'm using
here is a basic water-fed pole

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:25.830
that most window cleaners would use.

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:28.020
That's all you need.
It's got four jets in it,

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:29.940
which will fire out the chemical mix

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:31.137
with the water onto the roof,

00:25:31.137 --> 00:25:33.390
and the brush is just used
to agitate that chemical,

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.220
which will help it foam
up, as you can see.

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:37.050
This helps it stick to the roof more,

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:38.910
as it's a natural surfactant.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:40.200
So, what I'm doing here is

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.810
I'm applying the biocide
solution to the roof.

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:46.230
Now, this is diluted at 25:1 with water.

00:25:46.230 --> 00:25:49.530
So for every 1 liter of biocide,
I use 25 liters of water

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:52.860
to dilute it, ensuring I'm
covering every square inch

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:55.170
of this roof, making sure
it's completely soaked in.

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:57.900
A lot of mistakes people make
is not putting enough on,

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:00.150
and they're left with a very patchy roof.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.580
So it's really important
to get complete coverage

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.350
and make sure all of the tiny little gaps

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:06.210
are completely soaked.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.670
So if you don't fill all
of the gaps on the roof

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:10.170
with the chemical, then the moss

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:11.730
will regrow with a vengeance.

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:13.890
The important part is
making sure anything organic

00:26:13.890 --> 00:26:16.950
on this roof is completely
absorbing this chemical.

00:26:16.950 --> 00:26:19.708
So once the biocide
completely soaks and dries in,

00:26:19.708 --> 00:26:21.090
you would never even know it was on there,

00:26:21.090 --> 00:26:23.550
and your results will start
to come after a few weeks.

00:26:23.550 --> 00:26:25.380
The main thing is
trusting in the chemical,

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:27.280
because it definitely will do its job.

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:33.570
So, it's important to
have your roof cleaned

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:35.520
because the buildup of moss will over time

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.403
damage the tiles on the roof,

00:26:37.403 --> 00:26:41.236
which can lead to cracks,
damp, leak, all sorts of issues

00:26:41.236 --> 00:26:43.620
in your home that you
really don't want happening.

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:46.590
And if the problem is
not treated early enough,

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:49.140
then it can lead to very
expensive roof repairs

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:50.190
in the future.

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:54.210
As far as timescale on how
often should my roof be cleaned,

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:57.210
it is pretty much a one-time
deal for the majority

00:26:57.210 --> 00:26:59.403
of the process, as long as
it's being kept on top of,

00:26:59.403 --> 00:27:01.980
and in future it would just
be a light chemical treatment

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:03.360
just to keep anything at bay.

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:05.220
So the first step is getting it done,

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:07.003
and then it's a case of maintaining it.

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:15.810
Narrator: Even the
household appliances we use

00:27:15.810 --> 00:27:18.270
to keep things neat,
like washing machines,

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:19.710
need to be cleaned.

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:22.380
Back inside the home, we see what it takes

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:25.440
to remove the buildup of
detergent and softener

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:29.250
that can wear down machines
if not cleaned properly.

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:31.620
Hi, I'm Derrick Dennis.
I specialize in repairing

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:35.673
and cleaning washing machines,
dryers, any major appliances.

00:27:37.740 --> 00:27:39.240
The two main types of washing machines

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:41.580
that I work on are top
loaders and front loaders.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:43.330
I'm going to show you how I clean them.

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:47.329
So, this top-load washer is, I would say,

00:27:47.329 --> 00:27:49.350
between 15 and 17 years old.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.300
This machine here hasn't been cleaned,

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.670
I would say, ever since it was purchased.

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:55.455
The biggest problem with this:

00:27:55.455 --> 00:27:58.950
the abundance of buildup from
excessive use of detergent,

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:01.290
fabric softener. All the buildup you see,

00:28:01.290 --> 00:28:02.997
that's what causes this
big ring around the top.

00:28:02.997 --> 00:28:04.470
The water doesn't come up that high.

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:06.390
That's why you see the different levels.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.190
And then the buildup of mold.

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:11.100
The perfect ideal circumstance for mold

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:15.210
and mildew buildup is
moisture, darkness, and heat.

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:17.938
So, common buildup in
these machines due to

00:28:17.938 --> 00:28:20.260
overuse of high-efficiency detergent

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:22.267
and fabric softener, which you don't need.

00:28:22.267 --> 00:28:24.291
This big buildup, you're
going to get rust,

00:28:24.291 --> 00:28:26.932
you're going to get mold, mildew.

00:28:26.932 --> 00:28:29.370
2 tablespoons of high-efficiency detergent

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:31.535
and no fabric softener
will make your machine

00:28:31.535 --> 00:28:34.767
last ages longer than if you're dumping

00:28:34.767 --> 00:28:37.220
a cup or two of fabric softener in it.

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:39.270
It doesn't get your clothes any cleaner.

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:41.370
I highly recommend cutting
out fabric softener

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.020
in your laundry regimen.

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:46.110
The reason that I'm so
against fabric softener is

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:48.750
when you use fabric softener,
the reason that it works

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:50.340
is the reason that it's bad.

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:52.710
So it builds up like an oily residue

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:55.170
because those enzymes have
been trapped inside your shirt,

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:57.060
and so that's what causes it to smell.

00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:58.620
So it's not because of you,

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:01.710
but eventually the overuse
of high-efficiency detergent

00:29:01.710 --> 00:29:03.270
and fabric softener
will cause your clothes

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:05.103
to smell even if they're clean.

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:08.100
So anytime we maintenance the
machine, we want to make sure

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:10.200
that we're unplugging it
from any power source,

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:12.000
and it's good to cut the water off.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:14.760
So generally we start
from top to the bottom.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.100
The main thing is removing the tub

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:19.320
and cleaning the inner and outer baskets.

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:21.453
That's where all of your smell comes from.

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:24.600
With these direct-drive machines,

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:27.030
a lot of times we have the legs are rusty,

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:29.730
and we'll either scrape
them down, repaint them,

00:29:29.730 --> 00:29:32.220
make sure we knock the cobwebs
out, all that kind of stuff.

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:33.870
Sometimes we'll find a little baby sock.

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:35.430
You never know.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:39.030
We like to use toothbrushes,
different-style brushes.

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:40.620
You have pointy brushes, flat brushes.

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:42.150
They all have different jobs,

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:44.610
but brushes are very key.

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:46.560
They're vital to everything we do.

00:29:46.560 --> 00:29:48.600
So the brushes really help to get in there

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:50.902
and kind of get in the
corners and the crevices

00:29:50.902 --> 00:29:52.863
to clean all this mildew and mold out.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:56.580
Sometimes the pressure washer doesn't get

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.750
inside the balance ring

00:29:57.750 --> 00:29:59.550
and we have to get some bristles in there

00:29:59.550 --> 00:30:02.160
and kind of really put
some elbow grease in it.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:05.820
My favorite cleaner is
Pine-Sol, bleach, Lysol.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:09.337
Vinegar is more of what I
recommend for people to use

00:30:09.337 --> 00:30:11.490
through a washing cycle.

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:14.340
White distilled vinegar
has acetic compounds

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.593
that help kill some bacterias.

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:21.030
When we pressure wash, first
we start at the outside.

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:23.460
We can get a lot better
into the balance ring.

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.770
A pressure washer, the
water finds its ways

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:29.340
into all the crevices, and
it does a much better job

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:31.052
at pushing out all this mold

00:30:31.052 --> 00:30:32.730
and mildew and detergent buildup

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:35.100
and fabric-softener buildup
all the way out the holes,

00:30:35.100 --> 00:30:36.690
and then we can just rinse
it out of the inside,

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:39.660
and we have pretty much
a brand-new basket.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:41.033
So the steam cleaning comes,

00:30:41.033 --> 00:30:42.787
usually comes after the pressure washing,

00:30:42.787 --> 00:30:45.690
just because we want to
get kind of a final clean.

00:30:45.690 --> 00:30:48.150
The best thing about the steamer is

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:50.190
the water comes out at
a superhigh temperature,

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:53.610
and that helps to effectively
sanitize any of these crevices

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:55.410
or cracks or anything
that we can't just clean

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:57.183
with a pressure washer or bleach.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:02.880
So, the front loader, the
cleaning process, it's similar,

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.430
but it's a lot different
in the disassembly

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.010
and just the orientation of the basket.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:10.920
I can't remove a basket all
the way from a front loader.

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:13.260
I can only clean the front half.

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:15.090
The old-school top loaders
are built like tanks.

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:16.380
The front loaders are not,

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:18.270
but they clean your clothes better.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:21.870
They generally don't need as
much cleaning as a top loader

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:23.190
because of the orientation

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:25.740
and the way the water spins inside,

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:28.590
and the water actually
keeps the tubs cleaner.

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:31.500
The biggest part of the smell
that we see is from pump

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:33.180
building up lint and debris in there

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:34.638
and people not cleaning it out,

00:31:34.638 --> 00:31:36.480
and then you stay with a smell,

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:38.310
or because people don't wipe out

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:41.100
their door seal every
time they use the machine,

00:31:41.100 --> 00:31:42.570
they keep the door closed,

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:45.270
and then that just
builds up a mildew smell.

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:46.650
As long as you keep the door open

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:48.870
and wipe the door seal out and

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.020
run a washer-cleaner tablet
through once a month or so,

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:53.250
white distilled vinegar.

00:31:53.250 --> 00:31:55.710
It's not going to smell.
I can guarantee it.

00:31:55.710 --> 00:31:57.156
So, with this front loader,

00:31:57.156 --> 00:31:59.700
it actually has a clean-out
spot for the lint.

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:01.050
But this is what protects your pump,

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:04.320
and this is what helps keep
the smell down internally.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:06.450
Pull the door down. We drain the water out

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.130
of this little, this is the drain hose

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.980
that actually pulls whatever
water is left inside the pump,

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:12.955
and then we take the
filter out, clean it out,

00:32:12.955 --> 00:32:14.454
and that's it. It's that simple.

00:32:14.454 --> 00:32:16.620
I recommend doing this every 30 days.

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:18.011
At least every six months.

00:32:18.011 --> 00:32:19.202
And this is going to prevent odor;

00:32:19.202 --> 00:32:21.060
it's going to keep your washing machine

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:23.070
lasting a whole lot longer.

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:26.220
The average lifespan of
pretty much any washer

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:30.021
says two or three years
before the bearings go out,

00:32:30.021 --> 00:32:33.300
and that really comes from
the overuse of detergent.

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:35.462
So when a detergent breaks
down that oil and grease,

00:32:35.462 --> 00:32:38.175
it'll cause the bearings
to get rusted, rusty,

00:32:38.175 --> 00:32:39.775
and then your washing machine will sound

00:32:39.775 --> 00:32:41.347
like a jet plane taking off.

00:32:41.347 --> 00:32:43.123
That's bad bearings. It's
really hard to get to

00:32:43.123 --> 00:32:45.258
if you can't physically
tear these things down

00:32:45.258 --> 00:32:46.500
and clean them yourself.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:48.870
So the best thing to do
is preventive maintenance.

00:32:48.870 --> 00:32:51.930
2 tablespoons of detergent,
no fabric softener at all,

00:32:51.930 --> 00:32:54.810
but if you have to use it,
make sure you dilute it by 50%.

00:32:54.810 --> 00:32:56.750
You know, when I sell
a machine to somebody,

00:32:56.750 --> 00:32:58.380
I want them to have the
faith that I have sold them

00:32:58.380 --> 00:32:59.706
the cleanest used washing machine

00:32:59.706 --> 00:33:01.203
that they can possibly buy.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:06.120
Narrator: Finally, we
have the dryer vents.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:08.588
These can become packed with lint and dust

00:33:08.588 --> 00:33:10.530
with regular use over time.

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:13.320
Here's how the professionals
tackle the job.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:16.170
My name is Aaron Cotton,
and I am the owner

00:33:16.170 --> 00:33:19.740
and operator of Dryer Vent
Superheroes of New Orleans.

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:21.510
My name is Sophia Giordano-Scott.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:23.430
I am the co-owner and business manager

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.773
of Dryer Vent Superheroes of New Orleans.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:28.620
Aaron: Today we're going to look at how

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:31.023
to clean out a dusty dryer vent.

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:34.680
The main thing that gets
basically conglomerated

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:38.430
in the vent pipes is a lot
of either dead skin cells,

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:41.580
pet hair, children's
glitter, regular hair,

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:45.690
just dust, dryer detergent,
anything that can stay

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:48.483
and stick on our clothes that
can come off in the wash.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:52.350
Sophia: The worst is always
going to be any pet clinics.

00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:53.760
You're cleaning towels daily,

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:55.020
and you're not just cleaning towels,

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:57.120
but you're cleaning
towels full of pet hair,

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:59.190
which is usually thicker and coarser

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:01.950
and sometimes more oily than human hair.

00:34:01.950 --> 00:34:03.840
Aaron: The metal brush
not only breaks it up,

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:05.580
but it kind of catches as well.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:07.140
So it caught all that hair,

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:09.027
and you can see as I pull it out,

00:34:09.027 --> 00:34:11.457
it is just almost a whole dog

00:34:11.457 --> 00:34:13.050
that it seems like I pulled out.

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:16.260
Sophia: It was literally just
mounds of hardened pet hair

00:34:16.260 --> 00:34:18.840
and lint inside, all the way to the exit.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:20.940
And then that exit had about 4 feet

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:23.610
of bird's nest just compacted at that end

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:25.397
because there was no
vent cover on the end.

00:34:25.397 --> 00:34:26.572
It was just an open tube.

00:34:26.572 --> 00:34:29.940
So it took us about two
hours to clean the vent

00:34:29.940 --> 00:34:31.792
in entirety, get everything out,

00:34:31.792 --> 00:34:35.190
and we scoped it with our
little camera about six times

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:38.003
trying to make sure we got
every little piece out of there.

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:41.580
Aaron: As the lint just
gets stuck in there

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:43.850
and it builds over time,
the heat can't get out.

00:34:43.850 --> 00:34:45.900
So the heat just stays there,

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:48.930
and it accumulates right
there, and it condenses,

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:51.633
and that's actually how a fire will start.

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.130
Sophia: Part of our inspection
when we first go in is

00:34:56.130 --> 00:34:58.830
to make sure, is this vent
connected within the wall?

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:00.420
We only use these types of vents.

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:02.610
They are metal but also flexible,

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:04.860
but they're the only type
of connection hose vents

00:35:04.860 --> 00:35:06.725
that can actually contain a fire,

00:35:06.725 --> 00:35:08.280
as opposed to the foil vents.

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:09.810
If you look on the back of your dryer,

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:12.510
most dryers will say,
"Don't use a foil vent."

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:14.580
And most people just
don't read that warning.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:15.840
Aaron: We get behind the dryer,

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:18.360
and we take the metal tube
that's behind the dryer,

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:20.910
and we take it off, and
we inspect to make sure

00:35:20.910 --> 00:35:23.670
that the inside is either dirty or clean,

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:27.123
because you either want to
clean it out or replace it.

00:35:28.590 --> 00:35:30.990
The second step would be to put our rods

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:33.750
with the brushes into
the wall with the camera.

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:36.780
We can see inside the
wall of what's going on,

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:39.228
and the customer can see as well

00:35:39.228 --> 00:35:40.950
either how dirty or how clean it is.

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:43.260
You can definitely tell
almost how long it's been

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.358
because if it's only been a year or two,

00:35:45.358 --> 00:35:47.610
maybe a little or nothing at all.

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:51.717
But when we're getting to the
three to five years or plus,

00:35:51.717 --> 00:35:54.637
you're getting literal mounds
of stuff that's coming out.

00:35:54.637 --> 00:35:57.210
You know, it's almost like
from a molehill to a mountain

00:35:57.210 --> 00:35:59.245
of stuff that you're getting
out and you're like, "Wow!"

00:35:59.245 --> 00:36:02.223
Sophia: It gets really
gross if it's 10-plus years.

00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:07.867
Aaron: So, after we inspect
it and see how dirty it is,

00:36:07.867 --> 00:36:10.080
then we take our brushes with our rods.

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:11.310
So that goes inside the wall,

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:14.013
and our drill basically
just cleans everything out.

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:18.690
Sophia: We call this
the vent-polisher brush.

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:20.940
It has these metal teeth at the end.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:22.440
So when that goes through your vent,

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.780
that's going to polish your
vents as well as clean it.

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:25.920
So it's going to make it even harder

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:27.520
for lint to stick in the future.

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:31.710
Aaron: When we take our
HEPA-filter vacuum cleaner,

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:33.760
we make sure that no dust gets in the air

00:36:33.760 --> 00:36:36.340
for either us or the
customer to breathe in.

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:40.020
No cleaning solutions or chemicals.

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:43.560
Just the brushes and the
HEPA-filter vacuum cleaner

00:36:43.560 --> 00:36:44.673
really does the job.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:47.953
And then once all that's done and clean,

00:36:47.953 --> 00:36:50.250
we turn towards the inside of the dryers.

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:53.130
We get all the lint that might
have been stuck in there.

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:56.220
We go back, and we take
our aluminum tube here,

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:59.010
and we put everything back
from the wall to the dryer.

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:02.940
Sophia: We do use heat-safe
tape on all of our repairs

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:05.640
or connection hoses, and
we do also use clamps.

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:06.810
The reason why we use both is

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:08.850
because you want your
dryer vent to be able

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:10.833
to be unhooked every year and cleaned.

00:37:10.833 --> 00:37:12.150
Aaron: And we push the dryer back,

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.760
and then we check under the lint trap.

00:37:14.760 --> 00:37:16.140
We've definitely found a lot

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:18.900
of weird stuff coming from the dryers,

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:20.280
especially under the lint trap.

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:22.590
I've had one employee as we were cleaning,

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:25.260
he pulled out a whole silver chain.

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:29.340
I've found money, like
$5 and a couple $1 bills.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:31.510
Sophia: And then, of course,
we get the not-so-fun stuff.

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:33.690
Bird eggs and wasp nests.

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:35.892
Aaron: That's usually towards
the outside of the house,

00:37:35.892 --> 00:37:38.130
not in the dryer itself.

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:39.270
Sophia: Also storm damage.

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:41.490
Whenever we have a bad storm, hurricane,

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:44.010
and something gets impacted in the vent

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:45.690
or the roof comes off or something,

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:47.460
you're probably going to
get water in your vent,

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:49.676
in which case you need a professional

00:37:49.676 --> 00:37:50.601
to come and take that out

00:37:50.601 --> 00:37:52.170
because it's going to
be really nasty water

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:53.570
mixed in with all that lint.

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.900
Aaron: So, we've had
multiple customers tell us,

00:37:57.900 --> 00:37:59.573
"I've been in this
house for over 10 years,

00:37:59.573 --> 00:38:02.340
and I haven't even thought
about cleaning it out."

00:38:02.340 --> 00:38:04.260
And they're like, "Why
are my clothes still wet?"

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:06.238
A lot of the times it's not your dryer.

00:38:06.238 --> 00:38:08.972
It's in fact that line is just clogged up

00:38:08.972 --> 00:38:11.040
with all the lint and hair and everything.

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:12.180
Sophia: The most important thing to do

00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:14.160
with keeping up your dryer
vent is definitely to get

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:15.660
that annual professional cleaning.

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.180
But in addition to that,
cleaning your lint trap

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:20.013
with every use is the biggest one.

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:24.150
Aaron: I would say the
most satisfying is leaving

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:27.270
with the customers all happy and smiling

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:30.930
and knowing that they're safe,
that their house is safe,

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:34.689
and that their product is going
to work as they want it to.

